        "product": "CH3CH2OH",
        "productName": "ethanol",
        "distractors": ["CH3CH2Cl", "CH3CH3", "CH3CH2OCH2CH3"],
        "reaction": "Acid-catalysed hydration of an alkene",
        "explanation": "Ethene adds water across its double bond in the presence of phosphoric acid, giving ethanol. Hydrogen chloride would instead give chloroethane, $\\ce{CH3CH2Cl}$."
      },
      {
        "reagent": "PCC, CH2Cl2",
        "conditions": "",
        "product": "CH3CHO",
        "productName": "ethanal",
        "distractors": ["CH3COOH", "CH3CH2Cl", "CH3CH2OCH2CH3"],
        "reaction": "Mild oxidation of a primary alcohol",
        "explanation": "PCC is a mild oxidant that stops at the aldehyde. $\\ce{K2Cr2O7}$ in acid would over-oxidise the product to $\\ce{CH3COOH}$."
      }
    ],
    "tags": ["hydration", "oxidation", "PCC"]
  },
  {
    "id": "chain-methyl-bromide-to-ethanamine",
    "title": "Methyl bromide to ethanamine",
    "chapters": ["Haloalkanes & Haloarenes", "Amines & Diazonium Salts"],
    "exam": ["JEE Main", "JEE Advanced"],
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "start": "CH3Br",
    "startName": "methyl bromide",
    "steps": [
      {
        "reagent": "KCN, ethanol",
        "conditions": "heat",
        "product": "CH3CN",
        "productName": "methyl cyanide",
        "distractors": ["CH3NC", "CH3OH", "CH3OCH3"],
        "reaction": "Nucleophilic substitution with cyanide ion",
        "explanation": "The cyanide ion attacks through carbon, so the nitrile $\\ce{CH3CN}$ forms. Silver cyanide would instead give the isocyanide $\\ce{CH3NC}$. Aqueous alkali would give methanol."
      },
      {
        "reagent": "LiAlH4, then H2O",
        "conditions": "",
        "product": "CH3CH2NH2",
        "productName": "ethanamine",
        "distractors": ["CH3COOH", "CH3CH2OH", "CH3CH2NHCH2CH3"],
        "reaction": "Reduction of a nitrile",
        "explanation": "$\\ce{LiAlH4}$ reduces the nitrile group to $\\ce{-CH2NH2}$, which adds one carbon to the chain. Acid or base hydrolysis would give the carboxylic acid instead."
      }
    ],
    "tags": ["SN2", "nitrile", "reduction"]
  },
  {
    "id": "chain-benzene-to-aniline",
    "title": "Benzene to aniline",
    "chapters": ["Aromatic Compounds", "Amines & Diazonium Salts"],
    "exam": ["JEE Main", "JEE Advanced"],
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "start": "C6H6",
    "startName": "benzene",
    "steps": [
      {
        "reagent": "conc. HNO3, conc. H2SO4",
        "conditions": "323-333 K",
        "product": "C6H5NO2",
        "productName": "nitrobenzene",
        "distractors": ["C6H5SO3H", "C6H4(NO2)2", "C6H5NO"],
        "reaction": "Electrophilic nitration",
        "explanation": "The nitronium ion $\\ce{NO2+}$ substitutes a ring hydrogen. Keeping the temperature below 333 K limits dinitration, and sulfonation needs fuming sulfuric acid."
      },
      {
        "reagent": "Sn, conc. HCl, then NaOH",
        "conditions": "",
        "product": "C6H5NH2",
        "productName": "aniline",
        "distractors": ["C6H5NHOH", "C6H5NO2", "C6H11NH2"],
        "reaction": "Reduction of a nitro group",
        "explanation": "Tin and hydrochloric acid reduce $\\ce{-NO2}$ to $\\ce{-NH2}$. Sodium hydroxide then liberates the free amine from its anilinium salt. $\\ce{C6H5NHOH}$ is only an intermediate of partial reduction."
      }
    ],
    "tags": ["nitration", "reduction"]
  },
  {
    "id": "chain-aniline-to-phenol-via-diazonium",
    "title": "Aniline to phenol via a diazonium salt",
    "chapters": ["Amines & Diazonium Salts", "Alcohols, Phenols & Ethers"],
    "exam": ["JEE Main", "JEE Advanced"],
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "start": "C6H5NH2",
    "startName": "aniline",
    "steps": [
      {
        "reagent": "NaNO2, HCl",
        "conditions": "273-278 K",
        "product": "C6H5N2Cl",
        "productName": "benzenediazonium chloride",
        "distractors": ["C6H5OH", "C6H5N=NC6H5", "C6H5NO2"],
        "reaction": "Diazotisation",
        "explanation": "Nitrous acid, generated in situ at low temperature, converts the primary aromatic amine into the diazonium salt $\\ce{C6H5N2+Cl-}$. The low temperature is needed because the salt is unstable."
      },
      {
        "reagent": "H2O",
        "conditions": "warm",
        "product": "C6H5OH",
        "productName": "phenol",
        "distractors": ["C6H6", "C6H5Cl", "C6H5NH2"],
        "reaction": "Hydrolysis of a diazonium salt",
        "explanation": "Warming the aqueous diazonium salt expels $\\ce{N2}$ and the ring carbon bears an $\\ce{-OH}$ group. $\\ce{CuCl}$ would give chlorobenzene instead (Sandmeyer), and hypophosphorous acid would give benzene."
      }
    ],
    "tags": ["diazotisation", "Sandmeyer"]
  },
  {
    "id": "chain-acetic-acid-to-acetamide",
    "title": "Ethanoic acid to ethanamide",
    "chapters": ["Carboxylic Acids & Derivatives"],
    "exam": ["JEE Main", "JEE Advanced"],
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "start": "CH3COOH",
    "startName": "ethanoic acid",
    "steps": [
      {
        "reagent": "PCl5",
        "conditions": "",
        "product": "CH3COCl",
        "productName": "ethanoyl chloride",
        "distractors": ["ClCH2COOH", "(CH3CO)2O", "CH3COONa"],
        "reaction": "Formation of an acyl chloride",
        "explanation": "$\\ce{PCl5}$ replaces the $\\ce{-OH}$ of the acid with chlorine. Chlorination at the alpha carbon needs $\\ce{Cl2}$ with red phosphorus (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky)."
      },
      {
        "reagent": "NH3 (excess)",
        "conditions": "",
        "product": "CH3CONH2",
        "productName": "ethanamide",
        "distractors": ["CH3COONH4", "CH3CH2NH2", "CH3CN"],
        "reaction": "Ammonolysis of an acyl chloride",
        "explanation": "Ammonia attacks the acyl carbon and displaces chloride to give the amide. Ammonium ethanoate, $\\ce{CH3COONH4}$, forms only when ammonia simply neutralises the acid."
      }
    ],
    "tags": ["acyl chloride", "amide"]
  },
  {
    "id": "chain-propanone-to-propene",
    "title": "Propanone to propene",
    "chapters": ["Aldehydes & Ketones", "Alcohols, Phenols & Ethers", "Hydrocarbons"],
    "exam": ["JEE Main", "JEE Advanced"],
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "start": "CH3COCH3",
    "startName": "propanone",
    "steps": [
      {
        "reagent": "NaBH4, then H3O+",
        "conditions": "",
        "product": "CH3CH(OH)CH3",
        "productName": "propan-2-ol",
        "distractors": ["CH3CH2CH2OH", "(CH3)3COH", "CH3CH2COOH"],
        "reaction": "Reduction of a ketone",
        "explanation": "Sodium borohydride reduces the carbonyl group of a ketone to a secondary alcohol. A primary alcohol such as $\\ce{CH3CH2CH2OH}$ would require an aldehyde as the starting material."
      },
      {
        "reagent": "conc. H2SO4",
        "conditions": "443 K",
        "product": "CH3CH=CH2",
        "productName": "propene",
        "distractors": ["CH3CH2CH3", "CH3CH2CHO", "(CH3)2CHOCH(CH3)2"],
        "reaction": "Dehydration of an alcohol",
        "explanation": "Heating with concentrated acid eliminates water to give the alkene. At lower temperatures the ether $\\ce{(CH3)2CHOCH(CH3)2}$ forms instead, and propane would need reduction, not acid."
      }
    ],
    "tags": ["reduction", "dehydration"]
  },
